UP Health
Comprehensive Build Execution Plan
Version: 1.0
Status: Ready for Implementation
Companion Document: UP_HEALTH_PROPOSAL_v2.md (read this first for full context on
what the tool is and why)
Builder: Solo Developer, $0 Budget
Date: June 28, 2026
How to Use This Document
This is the practical, step-by-step counterpart to the UP Health Proposal. Where the
proposal explains what the tool is and why each decision was made, this document explains
how to actually build it — broken into ordered phases, each with a clear goal, the pieces that
need to exist, how to know each piece is working, and what commonly goes wrong.
This document contains no code. It describes what needs to be built and why, in plain
language, so that any builder — human or AI — can pick it up and understand exactly what
to do next, without needing prior context beyond this document and the proposal it
accompanies.
If anything in this plan seems to conflict with the Proposal, the Proposal’s stated intent takes
precedence, and this plan should be adjusted to match it — not the other way around.
Overall Shape of the Build
The build is organized into five phases, each producing something independently testable
before moving to the next:
1. Foundation — project setup, network configuration, and the basic ability to read raw
permission data from any address
2. Permission Decoding — turning raw on-chain data into the plain-language breakdown
described in the proposal

3. Risk Analysis & Identity Gating — the flag logic, staleness indicator, and the
ownership-matching access control
4. Interface & Dual-Context Support — the actual user-facing experience, working both
standalone and embedded in a Grid
5. Polish & Launch Readiness — edge cases, error handling, and final checks before
sharing publicly
Each phase assumes the previous one is complete and working. There is no strict time
estimate attached, since the builder’s own pace and available hours will vary, but phases are
ordered so that the riskiest technical unknowns are resolved early, rather than left until the
end.
PHASE 1: Foundation
Goal
By the end of this phase, the project should be able to accept any address as input,
correctly determine whether it belongs to the LUKSO network at all, and successfully
retrieve the raw, undecoded permission data for that address directly from the blockchain.
Nothing needs to be readable or pretty yet — this phase is purely about proving the
underlying connection and data access works.
What Needs to Exist
Project setup. A basic frontend application scaffolded and running locally, using the
technology approach described in the Proposal’s Technical Approach section. At this stage,
this is just an empty shell capable of running and being built upon.
Network connection. A working connection to the LUKSO Mainnet RPC endpoint, capable
of reading arbitrary on-chain data. This is the foundation everything else depends on, so it
should be verified thoroughly before moving forward.
Address input handling, including the non-LUKSO address case. This is an important
addition to the build plan that does not appear in the Proposal, because it’s an
implementation detail rather than a product-vision concern.
Currently, UP Health is intentionally scoped to LUKSO Mainnet only, with no plans to expand
to other networks unless LUKSO itself follows through on any wider, publicly discussed
cross-ecosystem integration in the future. Given that scope, the tool needs a clear,
deliberate way of handling the case where a user pastes an address that is not a valid
LUKSO Universal Profile or Vault at all — for example, an Ethereum address copied from

somewhere else that happens to be correctly formatted but has no LUKSO-specific data
behind it, or simply a malformed address.
The tool should not silently fail, hang indefinitely, or produce a confusing blank result in this
situation. It should clearly and plainly tell the user that the address they entered does not
appear to be a valid Universal Profile or Vault on LUKSO Mainnet, and invite them to doublecheck the address. This check should happen early in the lookup process, before
attempting to decode any permission data, so that invalid input is caught quickly rather than
failing partway through a more complex operation.
This same handling should also gracefully cover the case where an address is correctly
formatted as a general blockchain address, but simply has never had any LUKSO-specific
data written to it at all — this is a valid, expected case (not an error in the underlying
system), and should be treated as “no profile found here,” not as a technical failure.
Basic raw data retrieval. The ability to fetch the raw, undecoded ERC725Y data associated
with an address’s permissions, using the standard data-key structure that LSP6 defines. At
this stage, seeing the raw, unprocessed values on screen (even in a technical, unpolished
form) is a perfectly good sign of success — the goal here is proving the read works, not
making it presentable.
How to Know This Phase Is Working
Given a known, real Universal Profile address on LUKSO Mainnet, the tool successfully
retrieves some raw permission-related data without errors.
Given a random, invalid, or non-LUKSO address, the tool clearly reports that no valid
profile was found, rather than crashing, hanging, or showing a blank or confusing result.
Given an address that is correctly formatted but has no LUKSO data at all, the tool treats
this as a normal “nothing found” case rather than an error.
Common Pitfalls at This Stage
Mistaking “no data found because this address has no profile” for a technical failure of the
lookup itself is an easy mistake to make early on, and worth being careful about — these are
two very different situations that need two different messages. Similarly, it’s worth testing
against a handful of different address types deliberately (a real Universal Profile, a real
Vault, a clearly invalid address, and an address from a different network entirely) rather than
only testing against one working example, since the error-handling behavior described
above is just as important to get right as the successful case.

PHASE 2: Permission Decoding
Goal
By the end of this phase, raw permission data retrieved in Phase 1 should be transformed
into the plain-language breakdown described in the Proposal’s Section 6.1 — a clear list of
what each controller can and cannot do, in everyday language rather than technical bitfield
values.
What Needs to Exist
Full controller enumeration. The ability to retrieve the complete list of every controller
address associated with a given profile or vault, not just check a single known address. A
profile may have any number of controllers, and all of them need to be accounted for.
Permission bitfield decoding. For each controller found, the ability to read its specific
permission value and translate it into the set of individual capabilities it represents. LUKSO
publishes an exact, unambiguous mapping between each permission bit and what it allows a
controller to do, and this mapping should be treated as the single source of truth for this
translation — there is no room for interpretation or guesswork in this part of the build, only
accurate implementation of a well-defined standard.
Plain-language phrasing. Once permissions are decoded into their technical meanings,
they need to be phrased the way described in the Proposal — as clear statements of what a
controller can and cannot do, avoiding technical jargon wherever a plain-language
equivalent exists. This is a presentation concern layered on top of accurate decoding, not a
replacement for it.
Vault support, applied quietly. As described in the Proposal, Vaults use the same
underlying data structure and permission model as Profiles. The same decoding logic built
for Profiles in this phase should work identically when given a Vault address instead,
requiring only a way to correctly identify which type of address is being examined (Profile or
Vault) so that any display labeling can reflect that accurately, without needing separate
decoding logic for each.
Basic profile display information. Alongside the permission breakdown, this phase should
also retrieve and display the basic profile metadata described in the Proposal (name and
avatar, where available) to make results feel human and readable rather than purely
technical.
How to Know This Phase Is Working
Given a real profile with multiple controllers, each controller is listed individually, with an
accurate, readable description of what it can and cannot do.

The decoded permissions match what would be expected based on LUKSO’s published
permission definitions — this is worth spot-checking carefully against the actual
standard documentation rather than assuming the decoding logic is correct just
because it runs without errors.
Given a Vault address instead of a Profile address, the same breakdown appears
correctly, with any labeling clearly indicating that a Vault is being examined rather than a
Profile.
Given a profile with only one controller, this is displayed clearly and accurately, without
error — this case is especially important to get right, since it’s directly relevant to a flag
introduced in the next phase.
Common Pitfalls at This Stage
Permission bitfields can represent combinations of capabilities, not just single ones, and it’s
worth being careful that decoding correctly accounts for a controller holding multiple
permissions simultaneously, rather than only recognizing one at a time. It’s also worth
testing specifically against a profile with a large number of controllers, since enumeration
logic that works cleanly for one or two controllers doesn’t always scale gracefully without
deliberate attention.
PHASE 3: Risk Analysis & Identity Gating
Goal
By the end of this phase, the tool should be able to determine whether a connected wallet
actually owns the profile being examined, and — only in that case — surface the full set of
risk flags, the staleness indicator, and actionable next steps described in the Proposal’s
Section 6.
What Needs to Exist
Wallet connection (initial, standalone-context version). A working way for a user to
connect their own Universal Profile via their browser extension, sufficient to know which
address they’re connected as. The full dual-context (Grid-embedded and standalone)
connection handling described in the Proposal’s Section 10.4 is more naturally addressed in
Phase 4 alongside the rest of the interface work, but a basic, single-context connection is a
reasonable and useful stepping stone to build and test the identity-matching logic against
first.
Identity matching logic. A clear, reliable check comparing the currently connected wallet’s

address against the profile address currently being examined. This check is the gatekeeper
for everything sensitive described in this phase — it’s worth treating as its own well-tested
piece of logic rather than something loosely scattered across the display code, precisely
because getting it wrong in either direction has real consequences: showing sensitive flags
to the wrong person is the exact failure mode the Proposal is designed to prevent, while
incorrectly hiding flags from the actual owner undermines the entire point of the tool.
Risk flag logic. Implementation of the specific flag rules described in the Proposal’s Section
6.2, each grounded in the research findings documented in Section 7 of the Proposal:
Detecting a controller that holds the ability to add new controllers or edit permissions, in
combination with broad execution or fund-transfer capability
Detecting the case where only a single controller exists on the entire profile, with no
separate recovery path
Detecting a Universal Receiver Delegate pointing to a contract with no verifiable code,
or one that appears unusually new
Each flag, when triggered, should carry the plain-language explanation of why it matters, as
described in the Proposal — not just a bare warning.
Contract-versus-wallet detection. For every controller, a basic check determining
whether the address is a smart contract or an ordinary wallet, as described in the Proposal’s
Section 6.4. This is shown regardless of connection status, since it carries no sensitive
implication on its own.
Staleness / last-activity retrieval. Using the block explorer approach described in the
Proposal’s Section 10.3, retrieve when each controller was last active on-chain. This data is
only shown once identity matching confirms the viewer is the profile’s own owner,
consistent with the rest of the sensitive information in this phase.
The one-line summary. Once flags and controller data are available, generate the single
plain-language summary line described in the Proposal’s Section 6.6, sitting at the top of
the results.
Actionable next steps. For each risk flag, a clear, concrete suggestion of what to do about
it, including a link out to the existing LSP6 permission manager tool already available in the
ecosystem, as described in the Proposal’s Section 6.7.
How to Know This Phase Is Working
When the connected wallet matches the profile being viewed, the full set of flags,
staleness information, and next steps appears correctly.
When the connected wallet does not match the profile being viewed — including when

no wallet is connected at all — only the basic, non-sensitive tier of information appears,
with no flags, staleness data, or next steps visible.
Each flag, when it appears, is accompanied by a clear explanation and a next step, not
just a bare label.
Testing against a deliberately “risky” profile setup (constructed specifically for testing,
not a real user’s profile) correctly triggers the expected flags, and testing against a wellconfigured profile does not trigger false positives.
Common Pitfalls at This Stage
This is the phase where getting things wrong carries the most real-world consequence, so it
deserves the most deliberate testing of any phase in this plan. It’s worth explicitly testing
the identity-matching logic against several scenarios: no wallet connected at all, a wallet
connected but viewing a different profile than their own, and a wallet connected while
viewing their own profile — each of these should be verified individually, not assumed to
work correctly just because one of them does. It’s also worth being deliberately
conservative with flag wording, since the Proposal’s success criteria explicitly call out
avoiding unnecessary alarm — a flag that reads as an accusation rather than a helpful
observation works against the tool’s own stated purpose.
PHASE 4: Interface & Dual-Context Support
Goal
By the end of this phase, UP Health should be a complete, usable interface, functioning
correctly both as a standalone website that anyone can visit directly, and as a Mini-App that
can be added to a user’s Grid on Universal Everything — with wallet connection working
naturally and appropriately in both contexts, as described in the Proposal’s Section 10.4.
What Needs to Exist
Full results display. Bringing together everything built in Phases 2 and 3 into the actual
layout a user sees: the one-line summary, the controller-by-controller breakdown, and,
where applicable, the risk flags and next steps — organized clearly and readably, consistent
with the tone described throughout the Proposal.
Context detection. A reliable way for the application to determine, at load time, whether it
is currently running embedded inside a Grid (with a parent page present) or being visited
directly as a standalone website. This determines which wallet-connection path should be
used, as described conceptually in the Proposal’s Section 10.4.

Grid-embedded connection path. When running inside a Grid, wallet connection should
use the mechanism LUKSO provides specifically for this context, allowing a visitor to
connect with a single click, brokered by the parent page itself, and correctly identifying
whose Grid the application is currently sitting in.
Standalone connection path. When running as a plain website with no parent Grid context,
wallet connection should follow the standard approach of connecting directly to a user’s
browser-based Universal Profile extension, the same general pattern used by any typical
web3 application.
Unified experience beneath two paths. Regardless of which connection path was used,
once a wallet is connected, the rest of the application — identity matching, flag display,
everything from Phase 3 — should behave identically. The distinction between the two
contexts should be invisible to the end user; it exists only in how the connection itself is
established.
Address input for the standalone context. In the standalone context specifically, a way for
a user to manually paste any address to look up, independent of connecting their own wallet
— since one of the tool’s basic promises is that anyone can look up basic information for any
address without needing to connect anything at all.
How to Know This Phase Is Working
The application, when deployed and added to a test Grid, correctly detects that it’s
running in a Grid context and offers one-click connection accordingly.
The same application, when visited directly by its own URL outside of any Grid, correctly
detects the absence of a Grid context and falls back to the standard browser-extension
connection approach instead.
A user can paste an address manually in the standalone context and see basic results
without connecting anything.
Once connected, through either path, the identity-matching and flag-display behavior
from Phase 3 works identically regardless of which path was used to connect.
Common Pitfalls at This Stage
It’s worth testing the Grid-embedded experience for real, by actually adding the deployed
application to a real Grid during development, rather than assuming the context-detection
logic works correctly based on local testing alone — the Grid-embedded context specifically
depends on being loaded inside a real parent page, which is difficult to fully simulate
outside of the real environment. It’s also easy to accidentally build the two connection paths
as though they were entirely separate applications; keeping in mind that everything past the

point of “a wallet is connected” should be shared, identical logic helps avoid duplicating
work or introducing subtle inconsistencies between the two contexts.
PHASE 5: Polish & Launch Readiness
Goal
By the end of this phase, the tool should be ready to share publicly with the LUKSO
community, with edge cases handled gracefully, error states communicated clearly, and
nothing about the experience feeling unfinished or confusing.
What Needs to Exist
Thorough error handling review. Beyond the specific non-LUKSO-address case already
covered in Phase 1, this is the point to deliberately think through and test other things that
could go wrong: a temporarily unreachable network connection, an address that exists but
has an empty or unusual permission configuration, a Universal Receiver Delegate pointing to
an address that no longer exists, and similar situations. Each should result in a clear, calm
message to the user rather than a confusing failure.
Tone review of all flag and message wording. A final pass specifically checking that every
flag, warning, and message reads the way the Proposal intends — informative and
constructive, never alarmist or accusatory. This is worth reading through as if seeing it for
the first time as a real user would, not just as the builder who already knows what each
message means.
Cross-context final testing. A final, deliberate pass testing the complete experience in
both the standalone and Grid-embedded contexts side by side, checking that both produce
a consistent, polished experience.
Verification against the Proposal’s success criteria. A direct check against each item
listed in the Proposal’s Section 12, confirming each one is genuinely met before considering
the tool ready to share.
Sharing and gathering feedback. Once the above is complete, sharing the tool with the
LUKSO community as described in the Proposal, and treating early feedback seriously as
input toward the Phase 2 ideas already identified and deliberately deferred in the Proposal’s
Section 8.
How to Know This Phase Is Working
Every success criterion listed in the Proposal’s Section 12 can be checked off with

genuine confidence, not just assumed.
A person unfamiliar with the project, trying the tool for the first time with no explanation,
can understand what they’re looking at and what to do next without confusion.
Deliberately feeding the tool unusual or broken input (invalid addresses, network
hiccups, unusual profile configurations) never results in a confusing or alarming failure
state.
Common Pitfalls at This Stage
It’s tempting to treat “polish” as a quick final step, but this phase is where a genuinely useful
tool can still feel unfinished or untrustworthy if rushed — particularly given that this project’s
entire purpose is to build credibility and trust within the community as a foundation for
future work. It’s worth resisting the urge to skip straight to sharing the tool the moment it
technically works, and instead genuinely sitting with it as a first-time user would before
inviting others to do the same.
